  "experiment": "E10",
  "findings": {
    "consistency": "Using k_f and (k_d-k_q) from the voltage fit, the torque law 1.5[k_f i_q + (k_d-k_q)i_d i_q] predicts the measured torque with scale 1.0014 and 4.00 Nm RMSE. The apparent 4.0% difference in the bare q-axis slope is absorbed by the reluctance term at the negative i_d operating points.",
    "convention": "Only the amplitude-invariant dq transform, with P=1.5(u_d i_d+u_q i_q), gives a plausible motoring efficiency. Using P=u_d i_d+u_q i_q gives a median apparent efficiency above 140%, which is impossible. The 1.5 factor is therefore fixed by the data, not chosen by convention.",
    "magnet_temperature": "The q-axis back-EMF coefficient falls at -0.122%/K, inside the NdFeB reversible range of about -0.11 to -0.13%/K. The copper-resistance term rises at +0.46%/K, within 17% of the 0.393%/K copper coefficient; the u_d channel alone does not identify R because the resistive drop is small against the cross-coupling term.",
    "torque_provenance": "The torque channel's fitted current slope is nearly temperature-independent (+0.022%/K on the high-load subset, -0.033%/K on the motoring subset), while the voltage channel shows -0.122%/K. The dataset description says torque is derived from set currents and voltages; the data are consistent with a fixed-parameter torque model and do not provide an independent thermal calibration of K_t. The voltage channel is the independent electromagnetic evidence here."
  },
  "hidden_pattern": "A dataset can be real and still not validate what it appears to validate. Here the temperature channels are measured, the voltage channel carries the expected NdFeB coefficient, and the power balance fixes the dq scaling, but the torque channel is model-consistent and temperature-flat. Treating its K_t as an independent measurement would overstate the evidence for thermal derating. The audit separates the independent channel (voltage/back-EMF) from the derived channel (torque) before drawing conclusions.",
  "power_balance": {
